cartridge "GUARDIAN/AHOY!/1986"

' GUARDIAN
' A ColecoVision game based on "Guardian", Ahoy! November 1986
' (Commodore 64 machine language at $C000, started by the two-line
' GUARDIAN BOOT loader). The program and the disk carry no author's name
' and none was found, so it is credited to the magazine. Made for Amy
' Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/guardian.dis, and watched in jsc64), the game is
' built for the ColecoVision: a canyon of nested ledges whose colour bands
' roll toward you, Earth and a dark moon drifting together in the night
' sky, a smooth two-sprite hover guardian with its shadow, saucers and
' pods that grow as they dive, an alien tower drawn in tiles, sounds.
'
' The game: alien ships rise from the far end of the canyon and dive at
' you, dropping two bombs each. Shoot them, but a shot only counts when
' your guardian flies at the ship's height (watch the gap between a ship
' and its shadow). Meanwhile the aliens raise a tower out there, one
' course every 16 seconds; when it is complete its red core is open for
' 16 seconds: hit it and Earth is saved (the next level is faster). Miss,
' and the tower fires: Earth is destroyed and the game is over. Three
' ships; a bomb, or a ship at the end of its dive, costs one.
'
' From the original (orig/): the main loop's pass (here two passes a
' frame, about what the NTSC C64 managed between raster line 256 and the
' next frame): one pixel of movement a pass in X 80-250 and height
' 170-215; one shot at a time, from the ship's middle, two pixels up every
' other pass (one above Y 140), drifting one pixel toward the middle when
' fired from left of X 122 or right of X 203, gone above Y 100; the alien
' ship moved every 5 passes (one less each level): rising from (171,138)
' to Y 120, then a dive of five 20-move stages (table at $C8DD: sizes,
' bombs in stages 2 and 4, a 12-pixel shift when it doubles in stage 3),
' steering one pixel a move toward your X from stage 1, until its shadow
' wraps to Y 8; its height 0-31 at random (pods always 5), hit only when
' height < (your Y - 170) <= height + 15; 50, 100, 150, 200 x 10 points
' by kind (the C64 printed a 0 after the score); bombs fall two pixels a
' move with the ship's steering; the tower: first course after 12 s, then
' one every 16 s, nine courses, the core open while the ninth stands, the
' tenth second-count destroys Earth; Earth and the moon move one pixel a
' second toward each other; three ships, no extra ships.
'
' Liberties: pushing the stick up raises the guardian (the C64 had pilot
' controls: up moved it down the screen); diagonals and moving while the
' button is held work (the C64 moved only on a plain direction); collisions
' are box tests on the original sprites' drawn areas, the guardian's being
' its solid body (the C64 used its pixel-exact sprite collision register); the field is scaled to the ColecoVision
' screen (positions and rules stay in C64 pixels); an alien ship blows up
' while play goes on (the C64 stopped everything for 1.5 s); the ship
' speed stops rising at level 4 (one move every 2 passes; the C64 went on
' to every pass at level 5, where its bombs steered as fast as the player
' could move and could not be escaped); the canyon's colour bands roll
' three times a second (the C64 cycled them ten times a second); after the
' game the title (the C64 asked PRESS FIRE BUTTON TO PLAY AGAIN).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick flies the guardian (left,
' right, up, down), a button fires. On the title, up and down choose, a
' button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

